 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My Samsung dishwasher DW60M6050FS keeps showing 4C and beeping, won't do anything"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "The 4C code typically indicates a water supply issue where little or no fresh water is entering the machine. This could be due to a clogged inlet filter, a kinked hose, or simply the tap being closed. To check this safely, please switch off and unplug the dishwasher first. Then, locate the inlet hose at the back of the machine and ensure it isn't kinked or blocked, and verify that your water tap is fully open."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok, I've unplugged it. The hose has no kinks. I'm not sure what you mean by the tap though, there's a small valve under the sink with a screwdriver slot, and it's turned at 90 degrees. Is that what you mean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, that small valve under the sink is exactly what I mean by the tap—it’s the isolation valve for your dishwasher. Turning it 90 degrees usually means it’s closed (perpendicular to the pipe), so you’ll need to turn it parallel to the pipe to open it fully. Since the code 4C suggests a water supply issue, please ensure that valve is wide open and then try plugging the machine back in to see if it starts filling."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, I've turned the valve so the slot runs along the pipe and plugged it back in. It's filling now and running normally. Thanks!"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That’s excellent news! It looks like the isolation valve was just closed or partially shut, which triggered that 4C code since the dishwasher couldn't get enough water pressure to start. To avoid this happening again, you might want to check that little valve under your sink occasionally if you notice any strange beeping or delays at the start of a cycle. Is there anything else I can help you with regarding your Samsung dishwasher?"
  }
 ],
